  "version": 2,
  "rules": "Non-negative whole frames at a reduced rational rate on a named clock. Big integers are decimal strings. Drop-frame is explicit. Instants use half-open containment. Seconds are the first whole millisecond inside the frame, with three decimals. Ticks require whole ticks per frame. No declared video rate means a 1000/1 millisecond source, no video frames or timecode. Hours do not wrap. Approximate times cannot enter these conversions. Seconds are explicitly derived reading values, never the exact reconstruction path; use frames at the rate. If a frame contains no whole millisecond, omit seconds and state secondsUnavailable, preserving the other forms. For decimal seconds text, start selects the first boundary at or after the request, end selects the last at or before it, and instant selects the containing frame. The edge is required. Movement is frame start minus asked seconds as a reduced signed fraction. Premiere whole milliseconds are the floor of the frame start. Read them only when exactly one frame writes that value. Both Premiere conversions require video rates of at most 1000 fps.",
